---
import GuidePage from '../components/GuidePage.astro';
import CalculatorIsland from '../components/CalculatorIsland.astro';
---

<GuidePage
	title="CKD-EPI Calculator: 2021 eGFR Equation, Step by Step"
	description="Calculate eGFR with the race-free CKD-EPI 2021 equation from creatinine, age, and sex. Compare it with CKD-EPI 2009 and MDRD, with a worked example."
	h1="CKD-EPI calculator: the 2021 eGFR equation"
	path="/ckd-epi-calculator"
	crumb="CKD-EPI Calculator"
	kind="tool"
	modified="2026-11-03"
	related={['/ckd-stages', '/egfr-results', '/cystatin-c-gfr-calculator', '/methodology']}
>
	<Fragment slot="intro">
		<p>
			CKD-EPI 2021 is the equation the National Kidney Foundation and American Society of Nephrology
			recommend for estimating GFR in US adults. It needs only serum creatinine, age, and sex, and it
			does not use race. Enter your values to see your eGFR and each step of the calculation.
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<CalculatorIsland slot="tool" initialFormula="ckd-epi-2021" />

	<h2>The CKD-EPI 2021 equation</h2>
	<pre>eGFR = 142 × min(Scr/κ, 1)^α × max(Scr/κ, 1)^−1.200 × 0.9938^Age × 1.012 [if female]

Scr = serum creatinine in mg/dL
κ = 0.7 (female), 0.9 (male)
α = −0.241 (female), −0.302 (male)</pre>

	<h3>Worked example</h3>
	<p>A 55-year-old man with a serum creatinine of 1.2 mg/dL:</p>
	<ol>
		<li>Scr/κ = 1.2 ÷ 0.9 = 1.3333</li>
		<li>min(1.3333, 1)^−0.302 = 1, because the smaller value is 1</li>
		<li>max(1.3333, 1)^−1.200 = 0.7081</li>
		<li>0.9938^55 = 0.7103</li>
		<li>142 × 1 × 0.7081 × 0.7103 = <strong>71.4 mL/min/1.73m²</strong></li>
	</ol>
	<p>That result falls in <a href="/ckd-stages">category G2</a>, mildly decreased.</p>

	<h2>CKD-EPI 2021 vs 2009 vs MDRD</h2>
	<p>
		The same blood test gives a different number depending on the equation, so compare results only
		when they use the same one. These examples leave out the race coefficients in the original 2009
		and MDRD equations.
	</p>
	<div class="table-wrap">
		<table>
			<caption class="sr-only">eGFR from three equations for two example patients, in mL/min/1.73m²</caption>
			<thead>
				<tr><th scope="col">Patient</th><th scope="col">CKD-EPI 2021</th><th scope="col">CKD-EPI 2009</th><th scope="col">MDRD</th></tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr><th scope="row">Female, 60, Scr 1.0 mg/dL</th><td>64.5</td><td>61.2</td><td>56.6</td></tr>
				<tr><th scope="row">Male, 55, Scr 1.2 mg/dL</th><td>71.4</td><td>67.7</td><td>62.9</td></tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</div>

	<h2>Why the 2021 equation dropped race</h2>
	<p>
		The 2009 equation multiplied the result by 1.159 for Black patients. A joint NKF and ASN task
		force recommended removing race from GFR estimation, and the 2021 equation was refit without it.
		Older lab reports may still show the 2009 figure.
	</p>

	<h2>When CKD-EPI creatinine is less accurate</h2>
	<p>
		Creatinine depends on muscle, so the estimate can mislead with very high or low muscle mass, a
		special diet, or severe illness. KDIGO suggests adding cystatin C in those situations when the
		result affects treatment. Our <a href="/cystatin-c-gfr-calculator">cystatin C GFR calculator</a>
		covers both the cystatin-only and combined equations.
	</p>

	<h2>Sources</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Inker LA, et al. New Creatinine- and Cystatin C&ndash;Based Equations to Estimate GFR without Race. <em>N Engl J Med</em>. 2021;385:1737&ndash;1749.</li>
		<li>Levey AS, et al. A New Equation to Estimate Glomerular Filtration Rate. <em>Ann Intern Med</em>. 2009;150:604&ndash;612.</li>
		<li>KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of CKD. <em>Kidney International</em>. 2024;105(4S):S117&ndash;S314.</li>
	</ul>
</GuidePage>
